This adds a `getPlatform() === CONST.PLATFORM.WEB` branch inside the `backdropStyle` `useMemo` to return a web-specific style shape (omitting `width`/`height`). Branching on the platform inside the component body mixes platform-specific styling logic into shared code, which increases maintenance overhead and bug risk. Note that a `Backdrop/index.web.tsx` platform-specific file already exists (and is edited in this same PR), so the web-only style behavior can live there instead.

Consider moving the web-specific dimension handling into the platform-specific `Backdrop/index.web.tsx` (or a `.web`/`.native` split of the style computation) rather than gating it with a runtime `getPlatform()` check, e.g.:

```tsx
// index.tsx (native): keep dimensions
const backdropStyle: ViewStyle = useMemo(() => ({width: windowWidth, height: windowHeight, backgroundColor: backdropColor}), [windowWidth, windowHeight, backdropColor]);

// index.web.tsx: no fixed dimensions
const backdropStyle: ViewStyle = useMemo(() => ({backgroundColor: backdropColor}), [backdropColor]);
```

(Note: this file already contains several pre-existing `getPlatform()` checks, so this may be an accepted convention here — flagging for confirmation.)

This hook now branches on a platform check (`getPlatform()` compared against `CONST.PLATFORM.ANDROID`/`CONST.PLATFORM.IOS`, stored as `isNative`) to decide whether to skip the attachment preview screen. Embedding platform-specific control flow inside a shared hook increases complexity and platform-specific bug risk. This codebase already resolves platform differences via file-extension splits (e.g. `getPlatform/index.android.ts`, `getPlatform/index.ios.ts`, and `.native.ts`/`.web.ts` variants), which is the preferred pattern.

Move the platform-specific behavior into platform-specific files instead of an inline check. For example, split the file into `useAttachmentPicker.native.ts` and `useAttachmentPicker.ts` (web), where the native variant calls `addAttachment(files)` directly in `onFilesValidated` and the web variant opens the preview screen. That removes the `platform`/`isNative` computation and the `if (isNative)` branch from the shared code:

```ts
// useAttachmentPicker.native.ts — onFilesValidated
const onFilesValidated = (files: FileObject[]) => {
    if (files.length === 0) {
        return;
    }
    // Native platforms don't show the preview screen.
    addAttachment(files);
};
```

This `Platform.OS === 'ios'` check branches the component into an entirely different render path (a native `<View>` overlay on iOS vs. the `<Modal>` on every other platform). Mixing platform-specific rendering inside the component increases maintenance overhead and platform-specific bug risk. The recommended pattern is to separate this concern into platform-specific files rather than an inline runtime check.

Extract the iOS overlay path into a platform-suffixed variant so the platform choice is resolved at build/resolution time instead of at runtime:

```tsx
// ParticipantPicker.ios.tsx — renders the absolute-fill overlay
function ParticipantPicker(props: ParticipantPickerProps) {
    if (!props.isVisible) {
        return null;
    }
    return <View style={[StyleSheet.absoluteFill, styles.appBG, styles.zIndex10]}>{pickerScreen}</View>;
}

// ParticipantPicker.tsx — renders the <Modal> path
```

This also removes the now platform-only `useThemeStyles`/`react-native` imports from the shared file.

The `getPlatform() !== CONST.PLATFORM.WEB` check embeds platform-specific behavior directly inside the component: web navigates after a double `requestAnimationFrame`, while all other platforms navigate immediately. Mixing platform branching into the component increases maintenance overhead and platform-specific bug risk. Separating platform concerns keeps the component logic uniform and easier to reason about.

Move the platform-specific scheduling into a dedicated platform-specific file (e.g. a `deferNavigation.web.ts` / `deferNavigation.ts` helper) so the component calls a single API without a `getPlatform()` branch:

```tsx
// deferNavigation.ts (native/default)
function deferNavigation(navigate: () => void) {
    navigate();
}

// deferNavigation.web.ts
function deferNavigation(navigate: () => void) {
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        requestAnimationFrame(navigate);
    });
}

// in the component:
const navigateToReceipt = () => {
    deferNavigation(() => {
        Navigation.navigate(/* ... */);
    });
};
```

The component branches its rendering strategy on a platform check (`getPlatform() === CONST.PLATFORM.WEB`) inside the component body, which mixes platform-specific behavior into shared component logic and increases the risk of platform-specific bugs. Since the group-splitting behavior is web-only, it is a better fit for the platform-specific-file mechanism the search list already uses (e.g. `BaseSearchList/index.web.tsx` vs `index.native.tsx`).

Consider deriving `shouldSplit` from a small platform-specific helper/file (or a `.web`/`.native` split) rather than an inline `getPlatform()` comparison, so the web-only split logic is isolated:

```tsx
// e.g. a shouldSplitGroups.web.ts / shouldSplitGroups.native.ts helper
const shouldSplit = shouldSplitGroups(!!groupBy && isLargeScreenWidth);
```

This change adds a runtime `Platform.OS === 'ios'` branch inside the component to give iOS sequential processing and other platforms concurrent processing. Mixing platform-specific control flow inside a shared component is exactly the pattern CONSISTENCY-1 discourages — it increases branching complexity and platform-specific bug risk, and the `.native.tsx`/`.web.tsx` exception does not cover an iOS-vs-Android split done via a runtime check. There is currently no `index.ios.tsx`/`index.android.tsx` in this folder, so the divergence is being handled with an in-component check rather than file extensions.

Prefer separating the platform-specific behavior through file extensions. For example, extract the asset-processing strategy into a platform-resolved helper:

```ts
// processAssets.ios.ts — sequential to keep at most one heavy HEIC decode alive
export default async function processAssets(assets: Asset[], processAsset: (a: Asset) => Promise<void>) {
    for (const asset of assets) {
        // eslint-disable-next-line no-await-in-loop -- decode one at a time to avoid OOM on iOS HybridApp
        await processAsset(asset);
    }
}

// processAssets.ts (default/android) — concurrent
export default async function processAssets(assets: Asset[], processAsset: (a: Asset) => Promise<void>) {
    await Promise.all(assets.map(processAsset));
}
```

Then call the resolved `processAssets(assets, processAsset)` in `index.native.tsx` without the `Platform.OS` check, letting the bundler pick the correct implementation per platform.

This new effect gates web-only behavior (`window.addEventListener('popstate', ...)`, `isInternalPopstateInProgress()`) behind a runtime `Platform.OS !== 'web'` check inside a cross-platform component. Mixing platform detection with platform-specific browser-API logic in a shared component increases complexity and the risk of platform-specific bugs. Browser `popstate` handling is already isolated in web-specific files elsewhere (e.g., `src/components/Modal/index.tsx`), which is the established pattern.

Extract this browser-navigation-close logic out of the shared component and into platform-specific files instead of branching on `Platform.OS`. For example, split `DatePickerModal` into `DatePickerModal/index.tsx` (web) which registers the `popstate` listener, and `DatePickerModal/index.native.tsx` (native) which renders without it — letting the bundler pick the correct implementation and removing the runtime `Platform.OS` check:

```tsx
// DatePickerModal/index.tsx (web)
useEffect(() => {
    if (!isSmallScreenWidth || !isVisible || !shouldCloseWhenBrowserNavigationChanged) {
        return;
    }
    const listener = () => {
        if (isInternalPopstateInProgress()) {
            return;
        }
        onClose?.();
    };
    window.addEventListener('popstate', listener);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('popstate', listener);
}, [isSmallScreenWidth, isVisible, onClose, shouldCloseWhenBrowserNavigationChanged]);

// DatePickerModal/index.native.tsx — no popstate effect
```

`getPlatform()` is called inside the component to conditionally apply `contentContainerDirectionStyle` only on web. This mixes platform-specific logic into the component body. Since `ActionSheetAwareScrollView` already uses platform-specific file extensions (`index.ios.tsx`, `index.android.tsx`, `index.tsx`), `InvertedFlatList` should follow the same pattern with a `index.native.tsx` (no direction styles) and `index.tsx` (web, with direction styles) rather than using a runtime platform check.

Create platform-specific file variants:
```tsx
// index.tsx (web) - applies direction styles unconditionally
const contentContainerDirectionStyle = [restProps.horizontal ? styles.flexRowReverse : styles.flexColumnReverse];
// ...
contentContainerStyle={[restProps.contentContainerStyle, contentContainerDirectionStyle]}

// index.native.tsx - no direction styles
contentContainerStyle={restProps.contentContainerStyle}
```

